    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `isis enable 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nThe command enables IS-IS (with instance 1) on the specified interfaces in Juniper\\'s syntax. Note:\\n1. Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-X/Y/Z instead of GigabitEthernet)\\n2. Juniper requires interface unit numbers (.0 is commonly used)\\n3. The IS-IS instance is implied in Juniper rather than explicitly numbered like HUAWEI\\'s \"1\"', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `isis enable 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nThe command enables IS-IS (with instance 1) on the specified interfaces in Juniper\\'s syntax. Note:\\n1. Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-X/Y/Z instead of GigabitEthernet)\\n2. Juniper requires interface unit numbers (.0 is commonly used)\\n3. The IS-IS instance is implied in Juniper rather than explicitly numbered like HUAWEI\\'s \"1\"', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['ip address unnumbered interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet\\nset interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 tunnel unnumbered-inet-protection lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses a different approach for unnumbered interfaces. The equivalent is to configure the tunnel interface to use the loopback\\'s address as its unnumbered source.\\n2. \"gr-1/0/0\" represents a generic tunnel interface in Juniper (equivalent to Tunnel1 in Huawei). The actual interface name may vary based on Juniper platform.\\n3. The first command ensures the loopback has an address, while the second configures the tunnel to use it.\\n\\nAlternative (if using IP unnumbered directly):\\n##\\nset interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nChoose the appropriate version based on your specific JunOS version and tunnel type.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 tunnel unnumbered-inet-protection lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet\\nset interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 tunnel unnumbered-inet-protection lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses a different approach for unnumbered interfaces. The equivalent is to configure the tunnel interface to use the loopback\\'s address as its unnumbered source.\\n2. \"gr-1/0/0\" represents a generic tunnel interface in Juniper (equivalent to Tunnel1 in Huawei). The actual interface name may vary based on Juniper platform.\\n3. The first command ensures the loopback has an address, while the second configures the tunnel to use it.\\n\\nAlternative (if using IP unnumbered directly):\\n##\\nset interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nChoose the appropriate version based on your specific JunOS version and tunnel type.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 tunnel unnumbered-inet-protection lo0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet\\nset interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 tunnel unnumbered-inet-protection lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses a different approach for unnumbered interfaces. The equivalent is to configure the tunnel interface to use the loopback\\'s address as its unnumbered source.\\n2. \"gr-1/0/0\" represents a generic tunnel interface in Juniper (equivalent to Tunnel1 in Huawei). The actual interface name may vary based on Juniper platform.\\n3. The first command ensures the loopback has an address, while the second configures the tunnel to use it.\\n\\nAlternative (if using IP unnumbered directly):\\n##\\nset interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nChoose the appropriate version based on your specific JunOS version and tunnel type.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces gr-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol mpls te', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface tunnel1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls interface tunnel1\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you need a more precise Juniper equivalent that specifically sets the tunnel protocol to MPLS-TE (as the original Huawei command does), you would use:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls traffic-engineering interface tunnel1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, MPLS TE tunnels are typically configured under `protocols mpls` with TE-specific parameters, rather than having a direct \"tunnel-protocol\" command like Huawei. The interface is enabled for MPLS TE rather than explicitly setting a tunnel protocol type.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface tunnel1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls traffic-engineering interface tunnel1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls interface tunnel1\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you need a more precise Juniper equivalent that specifically sets the tunnel protocol to MPLS-TE (as the original Huawei command does), you would use:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls traffic-engineering interface tunnel1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, MPLS TE tunnels are typically configured under `protocols mpls` with TE-specific parameters, rather than having a direct \"tunnel-protocol\" command like Huawei. The interface is enabled for MPLS TE rather than explicitly setting a tunnel protocol type.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls traffic-engineering interface tunnel1'}]]": 1,
    "['destination [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path LSP-to-PE3 to 3.3.3.3', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `destination 3.3.3.3`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path LSP-to-PE3 to 3.3.3.3\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would typically create a Label Switched Path (LSP) with a name (I used \"LSP-to-PE3\" as an example) and specify the destination address with the `to` statement. The exact LSP name would depend on your naming conventions.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path LSP-to-PE3 to 3.3.3.3'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te signal-protocol segment-routing', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls no-cspf segment-routing', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for `mpls te signal-protocol segment-routing`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls no-cspf segment-routing\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling Segment Routing for MPLS TE is typically done by configuring segment-routing under MPLS protocols. The exact implementation may vary slightly depending on Junos OS version, and additional parameters might be needed for a complete TE configuration. The `no-cspf` option is often used since CSPF (Constraint-based Shortest Path First) isn't typically used with Segment Routing paths.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls no-cspf segment-routing'}]]": 1,
